Suppose $p$ divides $a b$ but $p divides.not a$. Since $p$ is prime, we then have $gcd(p, a) = 1$. By Bézout's identity there exist $x, y in ZZ$ with $p x + a y = 1$. Multiplying both sides by $b$ gives
$ b = p (x b) + (a b) y. $
Since $p$ divides $p (x b)$ and also the product $a b$, both summands above are multiples of $p$, so $p$ divides $b$. The other case is similar.
